WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
Inheritance questions in the HSC rarely use peas.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.160
They give you an animal you haven't met,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.880
or a family where the pattern isn't obvious,

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.360
and ask for a probability, or for what

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.800
the evidence can and can't tell you.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.640
Every one starts the same way: choose the

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.680
model, write the genotypes you're sure of, then

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.360
combine the chances.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.240
Here are four HSC-style questions.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.200
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.960
it through one step at a time, and

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.640
look at the classic mistake.

00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:32.946
Question one.

00:00:33.227 --> 00:00:36.426
In Andalusian chickens, a black bird crossed with

00:00:36.507 --> 00:00:38.986
a splashed white bird gives chicks that are

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:39.786
all blue.

00:00:40.427 --> 00:00:43.026
A farmer crosses two blue chickens.

00:00:43.307 --> 00:00:45.466
Explain why some of their chicks are black

00:00:45.627 --> 00:00:48.386
and some are white, and give the ratio.

00:00:48.747 --> 00:00:51.306
Then find the probability that their first two

00:00:51.387 --> 00:00:52.746
chicks are both blue.

00:00:53.387 --> 00:00:56.506
Finally, which cross would give only blue chicks?

00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:58.346
Pause here to answer.

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:10.446
First, choose the model.

00:01:10.927 --> 00:01:13.646
Black crossed with white gives a third colour,

00:01:13.727 --> 00:01:16.886
in between, so this is incomplete dominance.

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:19.966
A blue bird is heterozygous, with one black

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:21.646
allele and one white allele.

00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:24.526
Two blue parents each pass on one or

00:01:24.607 --> 00:01:27.326
the other, so the square gives 1 black,

00:01:27.407 --> 00:01:29.006
2 blue, 1 white.

00:01:30.234 --> 00:01:32.093
Each chick has a one in two chance

00:01:32.154 --> 00:01:34.633
of being blue, and each chick is a

00:01:34.714 --> 00:01:35.873
separate chance.

00:01:36.154 --> 00:01:38.393
So for the first chick and the second,

00:01:38.474 --> 00:01:41.593
multiply: one half times one half is one

00:01:41.674 --> 00:01:42.513
in four.

00:01:42.794 --> 00:01:45.273
And for only blue chicks, cross black with

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:48.553
white: every chick gets one of each allele.

00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:52.646
The classic mistake is crossing blue with blue

00:01:52.807 --> 00:01:54.086
to get blue chicks.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:57.446
Blue birds are heterozygous, so they can't breed

00:01:57.527 --> 00:01:58.006
true.

00:01:58.407 --> 00:02:00.086
The alleles separate again.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.180
Question two.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.300
In cats, a gene for coat colour is

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:06.660
on the X chromosome.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.780
One allele gives orange fur, and the other

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.660
gives black.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.300
A cat with one of each shows both

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.900
colours, in patches: this is called tortoiseshell.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:21.220
Explain why tortoiseshell cats are almost always female.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.820
A tortoiseshell female then mates with an orange

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:25.580
male.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.860
What's the probability that a kitten is a

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.220
tortoiseshell female?

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.180
And what fraction of their sons are black?

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.780
Pause here to answer.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.120
The gene is on the X, and a

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.160
tortoiseshell cat needs both alleles, so it needs

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.160
two X chromosomes.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.720
A male has one X and a Y,

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.920
so his single X carries only one colour.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.280
That's why tortoiseshell cats are almost always female.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.700
Now the cross.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.180
The mother gives an orange X or a

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.380
black X.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.700
The father gives his orange X to every

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.660
daughter, and his Y to every son.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.260
So daughters are orange or tortoiseshell, and sons

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.020
are orange or black.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.140
Tortoiseshell females are one box of four: one

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.300
in four of all kittens.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.020
Of the sons, one in two is black.

00:03:26.234 --> 00:03:28.633
The classic mistake is treating the gene as

00:03:28.714 --> 00:03:30.713
if it were on an autosome.

00:03:30.834 --> 00:03:33.753
That gives tortoiseshell kittens of both sexes, half

00:03:33.834 --> 00:03:35.193
of all kittens.

00:03:35.274 --> 00:03:37.753
But sons only get their mother's X.

00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:39.846
Question three.

00:03:40.487 --> 00:03:44.566
Phenylketonuria, or P K U, is an autosomal

00:03:44.647 --> 00:03:45.766
recessive condition.

00:03:46.407 --> 00:03:49.046
In this family, a woman doesn't have P

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:51.446
K U, but her brother does, and their

00:03:51.527 --> 00:03:52.406
parents don't.

00:03:53.127 --> 00:03:55.366
Her partner doesn't have P K U, but

00:03:55.447 --> 00:03:56.806
his mother does.

00:03:57.207 --> 00:03:59.446
What is the probability that their first child

00:03:59.607 --> 00:04:01.206
has P K U?

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:03.926
And if their first child does have it,

00:04:04.007 --> 00:04:06.166
what is the probability for the second child?

00:04:06.647 --> 00:04:07.926
Pause here to answer.

00:04:18.334 --> 00:04:20.093
Start with the people you can be sure

00:04:20.174 --> 00:04:20.653
of.

00:04:20.894 --> 00:04:23.693
The man's mother has two recessive alleles, so

00:04:23.854 --> 00:04:24.973
she gave him one.

00:04:25.214 --> 00:04:28.173
He's unaffected, so he must be a carrier.

00:04:28.414 --> 00:04:31.133
The woman's brother is affected, but their parents

00:04:31.214 --> 00:04:33.933
aren't, so both parents are carriers.

00:04:35.134 --> 00:04:36.013
Now the woman.

00:04:36.494 --> 00:04:39.293
Her parents' square has four boxes, but she's

00:04:39.374 --> 00:04:42.573
unaffected, so cross out the affected box.

00:04:42.894 --> 00:04:46.013
That leaves three equally likely boxes, and two

00:04:46.094 --> 00:04:47.693
of them are carriers.

00:04:47.814 --> 00:04:49.613
So she has a two in three chance

00:04:49.694 --> 00:04:51.213
of being a carrier.

00:04:51.294 --> 00:04:53.453
If she is, each child has a one

00:04:53.534 --> 00:04:55.053
in four chance.

00:04:55.134 --> 00:04:57.613
Two thirds times one quarter is one in

00:04:57.694 --> 00:04:58.253
six.

00:04:59.467 --> 00:05:02.186
Now suppose their first child has P K

00:05:02.427 --> 00:05:02.746
U.

00:05:03.307 --> 00:05:05.866
The child needed a recessive allele from her,

00:05:05.947 --> 00:05:08.026
so that proves she's a carrier.

00:05:08.227 --> 00:05:10.746
Now both are carriers, so the second child's

00:05:10.827 --> 00:05:12.906
chance is one in four.

00:05:12.987 --> 00:05:14.986
The classic mistake is giving her a one

00:05:15.107 --> 00:05:17.226
in two chance of being a carrier.

00:05:17.307 --> 00:05:19.626
That keeps the affected box, which she can't

00:05:19.707 --> 00:05:20.026
be.

00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:22.113
Question four.

00:05:22.594 --> 00:05:24.913
This pedigree shows a rare condition.

00:05:25.394 --> 00:05:29.793
Of three patterns, autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive and

00:05:29.954 --> 00:05:32.673
X-linked recessive, which can you rule out?

00:05:33.234 --> 00:05:35.593
Justify with numbered individuals.

00:05:35.874 --> 00:05:38.913
Then explain why this pedigree can't decide between

00:05:38.954 --> 00:05:40.913
the other two, and say which is more

00:05:40.994 --> 00:05:41.753
likely.

00:05:42.034 --> 00:05:43.313
Pause here to answer.

00:05:53.734 --> 00:05:56.133
Test X-linked recessive first.

00:05:56.454 --> 00:05:59.493
Individual 2 in generation one is an affected

00:05:59.574 --> 00:05:59.973
mother.

00:06:00.534 --> 00:06:03.493
Under that pattern, both her X chromosomes carry

00:06:03.534 --> 00:06:05.813
the allele, so every son gets it.

00:06:06.294 --> 00:06:09.413
But her son, individual 2 in generation two,

00:06:09.494 --> 00:06:10.453
is unaffected.

00:06:10.934 --> 00:06:13.653
And her daughter is affected, with an unaffected

00:06:13.734 --> 00:06:14.213
father.

00:06:14.614 --> 00:06:16.853
So it's not X-linked recessive.

00:06:18.067 --> 00:06:19.826
Now look for the giveaways.

00:06:20.147 --> 00:06:22.706
There are no unaffected parents with an affected

00:06:22.787 --> 00:06:25.906
child, and no affected parents with an unaffected

00:06:25.987 --> 00:06:26.466
child.

00:06:27.027 --> 00:06:28.946
Without one, both patterns fit.

00:06:29.507 --> 00:06:32.226
Dominant fits if each affected person has one

00:06:32.307 --> 00:06:33.626
copy of the allele.

00:06:33.907 --> 00:06:36.546
Recessive fits too, but only if the two

00:06:36.707 --> 00:06:40.546
unaffected partners, individual 1 in generation one and

00:06:40.627 --> 00:06:44.466
individual 4 in generation two, are both carriers.

00:06:45.667 --> 00:06:49.106
For a rare condition, two unrelated partners both

00:06:49.187 --> 00:06:52.546
being carriers is unlikely, so dominant is more

00:06:52.627 --> 00:06:53.106
likely.

00:06:53.507 --> 00:06:54.786
But it isn't proven.

00:06:55.267 --> 00:06:57.586
The classic mistake is saying it must be

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.826
dominant because it's in every generation.

00:07:00.307 --> 00:07:04.066
That's a clue, not proof: recessive fits too.

00:07:05.267 --> 00:07:06.986
So here's how to start.

00:07:07.267 --> 00:07:09.906
Choose the model: does the heterozygote look in

00:07:09.987 --> 00:07:13.026
between, or show both, and do sons and

00:07:13.107 --> 00:07:14.146
daughters differ?

00:07:14.387 --> 00:07:16.986
Write every genotype you can be sure of.

00:07:17.187 --> 00:07:19.986
For someone who's unaffected, cross out the affected

00:07:20.067 --> 00:07:22.106
box before you share the chance.

00:07:22.307 --> 00:07:25.346
For separate offspring of known parents, multiply.

00:07:25.667 --> 00:07:28.066
And if there's no giveaway, say what fits,

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:29.426
and what's more likely.

00:07:29.747 --> 00:07:32.226
Next: how common an allele is across a

00:07:32.387 --> 00:07:33.506
whole population.

00:07:34.734 --> 00:07:36.173
Here are the key notes.

00:07:36.414 --> 00:07:38.333
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:38.414 --> 00:07:39.213
to copy them.
